---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const title = 'How we measure supplements';
const description =
  'The six measurements recorded the same way for every supplement on this site, where each value comes from, and what we refuse to guess at.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumb crumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Method' },
  ]} />
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-6 pb-16">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Method</p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-moss-deep">How we measure supplements</h1>

    <div class="prose-mr mt-6">
      <p>
        Every supplement page on this site records the same six measurements, in the same order, using
        the same rules. That consistency is the point. If ashwagandha and rhodiola are described by
        different criteria, you cannot really compare them, and neither can anyone quoting us.
      </p>

      <h2>The six measurements</h2>

      <h3>Dose used in trials</h3>
      <p>
        The daily amount, and the duration, used in the published human trials we cite. Where the
        trials disagree we give the range rather than picking a flattering number from one of them.
        If the human evidence is one small study, that is what this line says.
      </p>

      <h3>Dose in our pick</h3>
      <p>
        What the product we recommend actually delivers per serving, read off the current label. A
        product can sit well below the trialled dose and still be the best available option, and when
        that happens we say so on the page rather than quietly leaving the gap out.
      </p>

      <h3>Standardised form</h3>
      <p>
        Whether the label names a branded or standardised extract with its own trial record, such as
        Suntheanine or KSM-66, or whether it simply names the plant. This matters more than most
        buying guides admit. A trial run on one standardised extract does not automatically transfer
        to a generic powder from the same plant, because the compound ratios differ.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a label does not state the form, we record "not stated" instead of assuming. Plenty of
        unnamed material is perfectly good. We just cannot verify it, and we would rather you know
        which of the two situations you are in.
      </p>

      <h3>Third-party tested</h3>
      <p>
        Whether an independent programme has tested the specific product, and which one. NSF, USP and
        IFOS are the ones that appear most often in this category. Manufacturer in-house testing does
        not count here, because the point of the line is independence.
      </p>

      <h3>Other actives</h3>
      <p>
        Anything else in the capsule with a pharmacological effect. A theanine product carrying an
        undisclosed 100 mg of caffeine is a different product from a plain one, and stacked formulas
        make it harder to tell what is working when something does work.
      </p>

      <h3>Reported onset</h3>
      <p>
        Roughly how long before effects show up in the trials, since this is the question people ask
        after they have already bought something. Some ingredients act within an hour. Others need six
        to eight weeks of daily use before anything is measurable, and knowing that in advance
        prevents a lot of wasted money.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the values come from</h2>
      <p>
        Dose, form, third-party testing and other actives are read from the live product listing on
        the day we check it, and the date is printed on the page. Trial doses and onset windows come
        from the papers we link. Nothing on these pages is generated from a manufacturer's marketing
        copy.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do not do</h2>
      <p>
        We do not run laboratory assays. We cannot tell you whether the contents of a capsule match
        its label, and any site claiming otherwise without an accredited lab behind it is
        overstating what it knows. What we verify is what the label claims, what the evidence
        supports, and whether those two things line up.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also do not quote prices we cannot keep current. Retailers change prices constantly, and a
        stale figure is worse than none.
      </p>

      <h2>Money</h2>
      <p>
        Links to retailers are affiliate links, so a purchase can earn us a commission. Nobody pays to
        appear on a page or to be chosen as a pick, and the criterion for each product is published
        before the recommendation so you can check our reasoning against it. Where the cheaper option
        is genuinely fine, the page says that too.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">
        Read the full <a href="/disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> or the
        <a href="/about/">editorial standards</a> behind these pages.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
